.TH CHTTPSVR_RESP_SET_HEADER 3 "2026-09-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_resp_set_header \- set a response header
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_resp_set_header(chttpsvr_resp *resp,
                                        const char *name,
                                        const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets or replaces a response header.  If a header with the same name
already exists (case-insensitive comparison per RFC 7230), the stored
value is replaced in-place and the function returns immediately.

Name casing: when a duplicate is detected the stored header name retains
the casing supplied by the FIRST call; the casing of subsequent calls is
ignored.  HTTP/1.1 header names are case-insensitive on the wire, so
this does not affect correctness, but callers who read back stored header
names directly should account for this behaviour.

All three arguments must be non-NULL; passing NULL for any of them
returns ccol_invalid_args without modifying the response.  name must
also be a non-empty RFC 7230 SS3.2.6 tchar-only token: every byte must be
an ASCII letter, digit, or one of "!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~".  A zero-length name
has no valid on-the-wire representation; a name containing any other
byte (e.g. a space or a literal ':') is not itself a CRLF-injection
vector but still produces a structurally malformed wire line a strict
downstream parser could misread.

value must not contain a CR or LF byte.  Both name and value are written
onto the wire verbatim (as "name:value\r\n"), with no further escaping,
so an embedded CR/LF in value would let a caller that reflects
request-controlled data (a query parameter, a path parameter, an echoed
request header) into a response header inject arbitrary extra header
lines, or split the response into two, on behalf of whoever controls
that data (HTTP response splitting / CRLF injection).  Rejected with
ccol_invalid_args rather than silently stripped or truncated.

A "Connection" or "Content-Length" header is accepted and validated like
any other name, but is never sent verbatim: the server always emits its
own Connection header, reflecting whether the connection is actually kept
open afterward, and always computes and emits its own Content-Length
header from the response body actually written (via chttpsvr_resp_write
and its variants), since both decisions drive real wire framing and must
never disagree with what the client is told.

A name of "Transfer-Encoding" (case-insensitive) is rejected outright
instead of being accepted-but-filtered: this server never transfer-codes
a response body (every response is Content-Length-framed), so honoring a
caller-set Transfer-Encoding header is impossible, and letting one reach
the wire would pair it with this function's own auto-computed
Content-Length header over a body that was never actually
transfer-coded; an ambiguous framing (RFC 7230 SS3.3.3) an
intermediary that honors Transfer-Encoding over Content-Length could
misparse, the same class of hazard the Connection/Content-Length
handling above exists to prevent.  Mirrors chttp_request_set_header's
identical Transfer-Encoding rejection on the client side.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args if resp, name, or value is NULL; name is empty or
contains a byte outside the RFC 7230 tchar set; value contains a CR or
LF byte; or name is "Transfer-Encoding" (case-insensitive).
ccol_not_enough_memory if the underlying allocation fails.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_resp_set_status (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_write (3)
